/* gsl/linalg.pure -- Cholesky (and triangular-solve) bindings for pure-gsl.
 *
 * NEW FILE proposed for the pure-gsl module. Add `linalg` to the module list
 * in gsl.pure (next to `matrix`), and the C wrappers below to gsl.c.
 *
 * Rationale: pure-gsl today exposes SVD and least-squares fit but no
 * Cholesky/triangular solve. A direct solve of a small SPD system A x = b is
 * the common in-database kernel (e.g. the MAP/Newton fits in pg.ddx.io's
 * commit model, K < 20, solved many times per daily refit). Doing it in pure
 * Pure over Pure lists is O(n^4)-ish and ~1 s at K=18; a thin binding to
 * gsl_linalg_cholesky_* runs it at C speed (sub-ms), which is what makes Pure
 * competitive with PL/pgSQL for this shape.
 *
 * All matrices are Pure double matrices (dmatrix). The routines do not mutate
 * their inputs: they copy, factor the copy, and return a new matrix.
 */

using gsl;
using namespace gsl;

namespace gsl;
namespace gsl::linalg;

private extern expr* wrap_gsl_linalg_cholesky_decomp(dmatrix* A);
private extern expr* wrap_gsl_linalg_cholesky_solve(dmatrix* L, dmatrix* b);

public
  cholesky
  cholesky_solve;

/* .. function:: cholesky  a::matrix

      Cholesky decomposition of a symmetric positive-definite matrix ``a``.
      Returns the lower-triangular factor ``L`` such that ``a == L*transpose L``
      as a new double matrix (the upper triangle is zeroed). Throws
      ``gsl::linalg::not_spd`` if ``a`` is not positive-definite.

   .. function:: cholesky_solve  a::matrix  b::matrix

      Solve ``a*x == b`` for a symmetric positive-definite ``a`` (n-by-n) and a
      right-hand side ``b`` (an n-vector, i.e. a 1-by-n or n-by-1 matrix).
      Returns ``x`` as an n-vector (1-by-n matrix). Equivalent to
      ``cholesky a`` followed by the triangular solves, but done in one GSL
      call. Throws ``gsl::linalg::not_spd`` if ``a`` is not SPD.
*/

cholesky a::matrix        = wrap_gsl_linalg_cholesky_decomp (dmatrix a);
cholesky_solve a::matrix b::matrix
                          = wrap_gsl_linalg_cholesky_solve (dmatrix a) (dmatrix b);

namespace;
